# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/maps/capital_homes.grim
# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after "="; keep {tokens}.
# "# en:" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;
# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.


@event townhouse_dinner
# en: Every Word Noticed
title [20e244] = 字字都被留意
# en: {&Dinner at the townhouse means silver, candles, an uncle, two aunts and your mother at the head of the table, and every word you say weighed like coin. "So," says an aunt, "the academy. Are they teaching you anything useful, or only magic?"|Dinner again, and the aunts again, comparing you, gently, with a cousin who has just married well. The soup is excellent. Nobody tastes it.|Another dinner, and your mother proposes a toast to the family's name and looks at you while she does it, which is either a compliment or an instruction.}
text [5e913e] = {&宅邸里的晚餐意味着银器、烛台、一位叔叔、两位姑妈，还有坐在主位的母亲；你说的每个字都像钱币一样被掂量。“那么，”一位姑妈说，“学院里教你们点有用的东西没有，还是只教魔法？”|又是晚餐，又是姑妈们，温和地拿你和一位刚刚嫁得很好的表亲比较。汤很好喝，却没人尝出味道。|又一次晚餐，母亲举杯敬家族之名，举杯时看着你——这要么是夸奖，要么是命令。}
# en: A candlelit formal dining room in an uptown townhouse, silver on the table, elegant older relatives watching a student at the middle of the table.
scene [6b01d4] = 上城宅邸里烛光摇曳的正式餐厅，桌上摆着银器，几位衣着考究的年长亲戚注视着坐在长桌中间的学生。
# en: Say all the right things
opt.left.label [ec5fb7] = 说所有该说的话
# en: {&You answer like a diplomat for three courses, modest, witty and never quotable. The aunts approve. Your face aches from the effort of it, and so does something else.|You praise the cousin, the match and the soup, in that order. Your mother's eyebrow says well done. The rest of you is exhausted.|You return the toast with a little speech so correct it might have been engraved. Your mother nods, once, and it will be repeated in letters for a month. You go up to bed hollowed out.}
opt.left.say.1 [a38098] = {&三道菜下来，你答得像个外交官：谦逊、风趣，却没有一句能被引用。姑妈们很满意。你的脸笑得发酸，心里也有什么跟着发酸。|你依次称赞了表亲、那门亲事和那道汤。母亲的眉毛在说：做得好。你其余的部分已经精疲力竭。|你回敬了一段正确得仿佛刻在碑上的祝酒词。母亲点了一下头，这件事会在信里被提上一个月。你上楼睡觉时，只觉得被掏空了。}
# en: Say something true
opt.right.label [6c6cfc] = 说句真话
# en: {&"They're teaching me to think," you say. "It's very inconvenient." Silence; then one uncle laughs. Next morning the pocket money your mother leaves on the hall table for you is a little smaller, for reasons nobody gives.|You say you're glad for the cousin but you'd rather choose for yourself. The aunts look at your mother. Your mother looks at the soup. The purse she hands you for the rest of the break is lighter than the one she'd shown you before dinner.|You raise your glass "to whatever the name turns out to mean". It's the best moment of the break, and it costs you: the theatre money she gave you this morning goes back into her reticule, without a word.}
opt.right.say.1 [40cc5e] = {&“他们在教我思考，”你说，“这很不方便。”一片沉默；然后一位叔叔笑了出来。第二天早上，母亲放在门厅桌上给你的零用钱莫名少了一点，没人说为什么。|你说你为表亲高兴，但你宁愿自己做选择。姑妈们看向你母亲，你母亲看着汤。她递给你这个假期剩下日子的钱袋，比晚饭前给你看的那只轻。|你举杯“敬这个姓氏最终会意味着的任何东西”。这是整个假期最好的时刻，也是有代价的：她早上给你的看戏钱，一言不发地回到了她的手提袋里。}

@event townhouse_calling_cards
# en: Cards on the Salver
title [dd5883] = 银托盘上的名片
# en: The silver salver in the hall is heaped with calling cards, and half of them are for you: word has gone round uptown that the family's student is home from the academy. A footman stands beside them looking hopeful. Every card is a call to pay. Every card is a door.
text [2050b2] = 门厅里的银托盘堆满了拜访名片，一半是给你的：上城都传开了，这家的学生从学院回来了。一名男仆站在一旁，满怀希望。每张名片都是一趟回访。每张名片都是一扇门。
# en: A silver salver on a hall table heaped with engraved calling cards, a hopeful footman standing beside it, a student on the stairs.
scene [570d96] = 门厅桌上的银托盘堆满了烫金名片，一名满怀希望的男仆站在一旁，一个学生站在楼梯上。
# en: Return the calls
opt.left.label [74ddcd] = 一一回访
# en: You spend three days in other people's drawing rooms drinking other people's tea and saying "the academy is very old" in every one. By the end your name is said kindly on four streets, and you never want to see a teacup again.
opt.left.say.1 [8ddd29] = 你花了三天待在别人家的客厅里，喝别人家的茶，在每一处都说一遍“学院非常古老”。到最后，四条街上的人提起你的名字都带着好感，而你再也不想见到茶杯了。
# en: Feed them to the fire
opt.right.label [407b6b] = 丢进火里
# en: You feed them one by one into the drawing-room fire while the footman watches in silent anguish. Uptown notices, a little, that you didn't call. The fire is very cheerful.
opt.right.say.1 [a4e210] = 你把名片一张一张喂进客厅的壁炉，男仆在一旁无声地痛苦着。上城多少注意到了你没有回访。炉火烧得很欢。

@event townhouse_suitable_tea
# en: A Suitable Tea
title [29542f] = 一场合适的茶会
# en: Your mother has invited someone to tea: a pleasant, nervous heir of twenty-two from a very good family, whom you are clearly meant to find suitable. They are clearly meant to find you suitable too. Halfway through the cake you catch each other's eye, and it's plain that neither of you wants to be here.
text [959059] = 母亲请了人来喝茶：一位二十二岁、出身极好、讨人喜欢又有些紧张的继承人，你显然应该觉得对方合适，对方显然也应该觉得你合适。蛋糕吃到一半，你们对上了眼神，彼此都明白谁也不想待在这儿。
# en: An uptown drawing room set for tea, a pleasant, nervous young heir in good clothes and a student exchanging a conspiratorial look over the cake stand, a watchful mother at the window.
scene [e71508] = 上城客厅里摆好的茶席，一位衣着讲究、讨人喜欢又紧张的年轻继承人和一个学生隔着蛋糕架交换着同谋般的眼神，一位母亲在窗边看着。
# en: Conspire to be unsuitable
opt.left.label [5060d2] = 合谋显得不合适
# en: In whispers over the cake you agree a plan, and spend the rest of tea being charmingly, impeccably unsuitable together: wrong forks, strong opinions, an unwise interest in goats. Your mother is furious. You've made a friend, and your mother takes back the money she'd given you for a new coat.
opt.left.say.1 [92bc2c] = 你们借着蛋糕低声定好计划，余下的茶会里一起表现得迷人而无可挑剔地不合适：用错叉子、观点激烈、对山羊有着不明智的兴趣。母亲气坏了。你交到了一个朋友，母亲也收回了给你做新外套的钱。
# en: Be politely dull
opt.right.label [ab6031] = 客气而乏味
# en: You talk about the weather for an hour. So do they. Your mother is delighted, and so is theirs, and at the door she presses a purse into your hand, unusually generous. You both leave feeling slightly smaller.
opt.right.say.1 [e3eb3a] = 你谈了一个小时的天气，对方也是。你母亲很高兴，对方的母亲也是，到了门口，她往你手里塞了一只格外慷慨的钱袋。你们离开时都觉得自己变小了一点。

@event townhouse_family_silver
# en: The Family Silver
title [60b95d] = 家传银器
# en: Late, going down for a glass of water, you find the old footman in the pantry wrapping the second-best candlesticks in a blanket. He goes white. "Your mother's instructions," he whispers. "The pawnbroker by the bridges. We do it most breaks, and buy them back when the rents come in." The house, it turns out, is poorer than it looks.
text [968f27] = 深夜你下楼倒水，发现老男仆在备餐室里把次好的烛台裹进毯子。他脸色发白。“是夫人的吩咐，”他低声说，“桥边的当铺。差不多每个假期都这样，等租金收上来再赎回。”原来这座宅子比看上去穷。
# en: A dim pantry at night, an old footman wrapping silver candlesticks in a blanket, startled, a student in the doorway holding a candle.
scene [620c1e] = 夜里昏暗的备餐室，一名老男仆正把银烛台裹进毯子，受了惊吓，门口站着一个举着蜡烛的学生。
# en: Keep the secret
opt.left.label [8c0aed] = 保守秘密
# en: You help him carry the candlesticks to the door and say nothing to anyone. At breakfast your mother is magnificent, and you look at the silver on the table and wonder which of it is coming back. The name, you think, is expensive.
opt.left.say.1 [7de2eb] = 你帮他把烛台搬到门口，对谁都没说。早餐时母亲光彩照人，你看着桌上的银器，猜哪些还会回来。你想，这个姓氏真贵。
# en: Give him your allowance
opt.right.label [e4b0f3] = 把零用钱给他
# en: You give the footman what's left of your allowance, to keep the candlesticks at home. He looks at you as if you'd grown. Your mother never mentions it. The next letter from home has an extra line in it, in her own hand, and no underlining at all.
opt.right.say.1 [9111f9] = 你把剩下的零用钱给了男仆，好让烛台留在家里。他看你的眼神，像你忽然长高了。母亲从没提过这件事。下一封家信里多了一行她亲笔写的话，一处划线也没有。

@event counting_house_end_desk
# en: The End Desk
title [d7351e] = 尽头的那张桌子
# en: {&The old clerk at the end desk has kept the firm's books since before your parents married, and he looks up as you come down as if you were a column that won't add. "This one," he says, and pushes a ledger at you. "Find me the error. There is always an error."|The end desk again. The old clerk has saved you a ledger with an error in it, the way a cat saves a mouse. He watches you over his spectacles and doesn't blink.|The end desk, and a ledger already waiting, with a peppermint beside it, which from the old clerk is either a kindness or bait. "Same as ever," he says. "Find it."}
text [50335e] = {&尽头那张桌子的老文书在你父母成婚前就管着商号的账，你下楼时他抬头看你，仿佛你是一列对不上的数。“这本，”他说着把一本账簿推过来，“把错找出来。错总是有的。”|又是尽头那张桌子。老文书给你留了一本有错的账簿，就像猫给人留一只老鼠。他从眼镜上方看着你，一眼不眨。|尽头那张桌子，一本账簿已经等着你了，旁边还放着一颗薄荷糖——在老文书这里，这要么是好意，要么是诱饵。“老样子，”他说，“找吧。”}
# en: A long counting room of high desks and ledgers on a harbour wharf, an old clerk in spectacles at the last desk pushing a ledger towards a student.
scene [a69e27] = 港口码头上一间长长的账房，高桌林立、账簿成堆，最后一张桌前戴眼镜的老文书正把一本账簿推向一个学生。
# en: Find the error
opt.left.label [51b13f] = 把错找出来
# en: {&Two hours of columns until your eyes cross. You find it, a seven that should be a one. The clerk nods, once, and writes something in a small private book.|You find two errors, and he's annoyed, because he only planted one. The other is real. He fixes it without a word and gives you a peppermint, grudgingly. Your head aches.|You find it in an hour, a decimal point hiding in plain sight, and your neck aches from the stooping. He takes the ledger back without a word and pushes the peppermint across the desk.}
opt.left.say.1 [4f0e7d] = {&对了两个小时的数，你眼睛都花了。你找到了：一个本该是一的七。文书点了一下头，在一本私人小册子上记了点什么。|你找到了两处错，他很恼火，因为他只埋了一处。另一处是真的。他一声不吭地改掉，不情愿地给了你一颗薄荷糖。你头疼。|你一个小时就找到了：一个明摆着却藏得很好的小数点，脖子却因为一直弯着而酸痛。他一言不发地收回账簿，把薄荷糖推到你面前。}
# en: Deal at the door for the firm
opt.right.label [59b7cb] = 替商号去门口谈价
# en: {&A carter at the wharf door wants double for the rope. You talk him down to fair and he calls you a robber, cheerfully. The firm tips you a coin, and the end desk a nod. It's exhausting.|A sailmaker with a sad story and a high price. You get the price down and the story longer. Your cut is small and your nerves are frayed.|A rival firm's man tries to buy the whole of your fleet's tea before it lands. You send him off with a worse offer than he came with, and the end desk actually smiles and slides you a coin for it. You're shaking a little after.}
opt.right.say.1 [5343c1] = {&码头门口一个车夫要两倍的缆绳钱。你把价砍回公道，他乐呵呵地骂你是强盗。商号赏你一枚硬币，尽头那张桌子赏你一个点头。真累人。|一个制帆匠，故事很惨，开价很高。你把价压了下来，故事却变得更长。你的分成很少，神经很紧。|对手商号的人想在你家船队的茶叶上岸前整批买下。你让他带着比来时更差的报价走了，尽头那张桌子竟然笑了，还推给你一枚硬币。事后你的手有点抖。}

@event counting_house_damp_grain
# en: Sound, More or Less
title [0c36f7] = 大致完好
# en: A ship came in this morning with half its grain gone damp. The firm has a buyer upriver, paid in advance, who won't open the sacks until they're milled. "Mix it in with the good," your mother says, not quite looking at you, "and nobody's the worse." She holds out the bill of sale for you to sign as witness. You're family now.
text [06df0e] = 今早进港的船，有一半粮食受了潮。商号在上游有个已经付了钱的买主，要等磨成粉才会打开麻袋。“掺进好粮里，”你母亲说，没太看你，“谁也不吃亏。”她把销售单递过来，让你签字作证。你现在算是家里人了。
# en: A dim wharf warehouse stacked with grain sacks, some dark with damp, a merchant mother holding out a bill of sale and a pen to a student.
scene [b608fd] = 昏暗的码头仓库里堆满粮袋，有些被潮气浸黑了，一位商人母亲把销售单和笔递向一个学生。
# en: Refuse to sign
opt.left.label [07438b] = 拒绝签字
# en: You won't sign. Your mother looks at you a long while, then tears up the bill, and the damp grain goes to the pig farmers at a loss. Your share of the season is smaller for it. At supper she says nothing, but she passes you the bread first, which she never does.
opt.left.say.1 [4d6533] = 你不签。母亲看了你很久，然后撕了那张单子，受潮的粮食亏本卖给了养猪的人。你这一季的分成因此少了。晚饭时她什么也没说，却第一个把面包递给了你——她从来不这样。
# en: Sign it
opt.right.label [4bdcb1] = 签字
# en: You sign. The grain goes upriver mixed with the good, and your share of the season is better for it. Nobody is the worse, probably. You keep thinking about the miller who'll open the sacks.
opt.right.say.1 [c493be] = 你签了。粮食掺着好粮运往上游，你这一季的分成因此多了。大概谁也不会吃亏。你总想着那个打开麻袋的磨坊主。

@event counting_house_violets
# en: A Room Full of Violets
title [05cd75] = 一屋子紫罗兰
# en: Your old room above the counting house is exactly as you left it, except that the firm has been storing something in it: forty crates of a very cheap violet perfume that nobody could sell. The whole room smells of violets. Through the floor you can hear the ledgers being kept, as you always could, the scratch of pens like rain.
text [6d9e47] = 账房楼上你的旧房间和你离开时一模一样，只是商号往里塞了东西：四十箱谁也卖不掉的廉价紫罗兰香水。整个房间都是紫罗兰味。透过地板，你能听见楼下记账的声音，一如往常，笔尖沙沙，像在下雨。
# en: A small attic bedroom above a counting house crammed with wooden crates of perfume bottles, a student sitting on a narrow bed holding their nose.
scene [6d43b0] = 账房楼上的小阁楼卧室塞满了装香水瓶的木箱，一个学生坐在窄床上捏着鼻子。
# en: Sell the violets on the wharf
opt.left.label [3cb88a] = 到码头上卖紫罗兰
# en: You take a crate down to the wharf and sell violet perfume to sailors, who buy it for sweethearts and, in one case, a parrot. You come home hoarse and fed up, smelling of violets, with a pocket of coins the firm says you can keep.
opt.left.say.1 [b7779d] = 你搬一箱到码头，把紫罗兰香水卖给水手，他们买来送给心上人，其中一位送给了一只鹦鹉。你回家时嗓子哑了、心烦透了、浑身紫罗兰味，口袋里有一把商号说归你的硬币。
# en: Sleep in the violets
opt.right.label [b66b74] = 在紫罗兰里睡觉
# en: You sleep, eventually, in a cloud of violets, and dream of nothing but flowers. You wake rested, sneezing, and thoroughly sick of violets, with the scratch of pens below already going.
opt.right.say.1 [c35a6e] = 你终于在一团紫罗兰香气里睡着了，梦里全是花。醒来时你休息好了，打着喷嚏，对紫罗兰彻底厌烦，楼下的笔已经沙沙响了起来。

@event crew_loft_long_game
# en: The Game That Never Ended
title [4bb95d] = 没完没了的牌局
# en: {&The card game in the loft has been going since before you left; some of the players have changed, and the pot hasn't. Nan Merrow, who runs the loft and most of the street, deals you in without asking. "The wizard," she says. "Show us what they taught you, or put a coin in."|The game again, the same chipped table and the same stove smoke. Somebody has carved your initials into the table's edge, with a little star, as a joke. "Coin or trick," says Nan.|The game, the loft, and someone you don't know asking who you are. "One of ours," says Nan Merrow, before you can answer. "Coin or trick."}
text [7c8696] = {&阁楼里的牌局在你离开前就开始了；有些牌手换了人，赌注没换。南·梅罗——她管着这间阁楼和大半条街——不问一声就给你发了牌。“巫师来了，”她说，“露两手他们教你的，要不就下一枚硬币。”|又是这局牌，同一张缺了角的桌子，同样的炉烟。有人在桌边刻了你名字的首字母，还加了颗小星星，算是玩笑。“硬币还是戏法？”南说。|牌局，阁楼，还有一个你不认识的人问你是谁。“我们自己人，”你还没开口，南·梅罗就说了，“硬币还是戏法？”}
# en: A smoky harbour-town loft with hammocks and a pot-bellied stove, a rough crowd around a chipped card table, a sharp-eyed older woman dealing a hand to a student.
scene [2b2473] = 烟雾缭绕的港镇阁楼里挂着吊床、立着大肚火炉，一群粗野的人围着缺角的牌桌，一位目光锐利的年长女人正给一个学生发牌。
# en: Put a coin in
opt.left.label [a16b4d] = 下一枚硬币
# en: Your stake goes in the pot.
opt.left.hint [563ee9] = 你的赌注进了彩池。
# en: {&You win a hand on nerve and a pair of nothing, and the loft cheers. Nan looks at you as if you'd paid a debt.|You win, and the loft groans, and somebody accuses you of using magic, which, for once, you didn't.|You win, and the loft bangs the table for you, and Nan pours you a drink from the bottle she keeps for funerals and weddings.}
opt.left.say.1 [6e5192] = {&你凭胆量和一手空牌赢了一局，阁楼里一片欢呼。南看你的眼神，像你还清了一笔债。|你赢了，阁楼里一片哀嚎，有人指控你用了魔法——难得这一回你真没用。|你赢了，整间阁楼为你拍桌子，南从她留着办葬礼和婚礼的那瓶酒里给你倒了一杯。}
# en: {&You lose your crown to a man with no front teeth, who says thank you every time. Nan laughs.|You lose, cleanly, to Nan herself, who has never lost to anyone in the loft. "Some things don't change," she says.|You lose your stake, and the loft cheers anyway, because you played.}
opt.left.say.2 [89c000] = {&你那一克朗输给了一个没有门牙的人，他每次都说谢谢。南笑了。|你干干净净地输给了南本人——阁楼里从来没人赢过她。“有些事是不会变的，”她说。|你输光了赌注，阁楼照样为你欢呼，因为你上桌了。}
# en: Show them a trick
opt.right.label [5e56fa] = 给他们变个戏法
# en: {&You make the stove smoke into a little dragon that chases the cat. The loft goes silent, then wild. Nan watches with narrowed eyes, as if pricing it.|You light every candle in the loft with a snap. Somebody starts to ask whether you could do that to a lock. Nan kicks them under the table.|You make the cards deal themselves. The loft is delighted. Nan isn't. "Don't go showing that round the docks," she says quietly. "People will want it."}
opt.right.say.1 [18200c] = {&你让炉烟变成一条小龙去追猫。阁楼里先是一片寂静，然后炸了锅。南眯着眼看着，像在估价。|你一个响指点亮了阁楼里所有的蜡烛。有人开口想问你能不能对锁也来这么一下，南在桌底下踢了他一脚。|你让牌自己发了起来。阁楼里的人乐坏了，南却没有。“别在码头上显摆这个，”她低声说，“会有人想要的。”}

@event crew_loft_one_job
# en: One Job, Like Old Times
title [9e0284] = 就一票，像从前那样
# en: Nan Merrow, who runs the loft and most of the street, takes you up on the roof, where the loft keeps its pigeons and its secrets. "There's a door on the wharf," she says, "with a lock nobody here can open. You could. One job, like old times. Nobody hurt, nobody the wiser, a fair cut." Below, Port Calder's lamps are coming on. She waits.
text [03ea13] = 南·梅罗——她管着这间阁楼和大半条街——带你上了屋顶，阁楼在那里养鸽子、藏秘密。“码头上有扇门，”她说，“那把锁这儿谁也开不了。你能。就一票，像从前那样。不伤人，没人知道，分成公道。”下面，考尔德港的灯一盏盏亮起。她等着。
# en: A harbour-town rooftop at dusk with pigeon cages, a sharp-eyed older woman pointing across the wharves to a warehouse door, a student beside her, lamps coming on below.
scene [8fc1c7] = 黄昏的港镇屋顶上摆着鸽笼，一位目光锐利的年长女人指着码头对面的一扇仓库门，一个学生站在她身旁，下方灯火渐亮。
# en: Turn her down
opt.left.label [d2ed8b] = 拒绝她
# en: "Not any more," you say. Nan looks at you a long moment, and then, surprisingly, smiles. "Good," she says. "Somebody from this loft ought to get out clean." She doesn't ask again. You feel as if you'd passed something, and lost something.
opt.left.say.1 [fd327e] = “不干了，”你说。南看了你好一会儿，然后出人意料地笑了。“好，”她说，“这间阁楼总该有个人干干净净地走出去。”她没再问。你觉得自己通过了什么，也失去了什么。
# en: Do the job
opt.right.label [573204] = 干这一票
# en: The lock takes you one breath and a little magic. Nobody's hurt; nobody's the wiser; your cut is fair and heavy. Walking back over the roofs with Nan, you feel twelve years old again, and not in a good way.
opt.right.say.1 [0f5af2] = 那把锁只花了你一口气和一点魔法。没人受伤，没人知道，你的分成公道又沉甸甸。和南一起从屋顶上走回去时，你觉得自己又回到了十二岁，而这并不是什么好感觉。
# en: The lock opens, and so does a shutter across the wharf. You run, the watch behind you, and lose your hat, some skin and the whole cut in the river. Nan hauls you in through the loft window. "Some things don't change," she says, not unkindly.
opt.right.say.2 [bf1a1c] = 锁开了，码头对面的一扇百叶窗也开了。你拔腿就跑，巡警在后面追，你丢了帽子、蹭掉了皮，整份分成也掉进了河里。南从阁楼窗口把你拽了进来。“有些事是不会变的，”她说，语气并不刻薄。

@event crew_loft_empty_hammock
# en: The Empty Hammock
title [d7569d] = 空吊床
# en: There's a hammock in the loft with nobody in it and a coat still hanging over it. You ask. "Jory," says Nan Merrow, who runs the loft, after a while. "Got caught at the bonded warehouses and the watch had him. The city gaol, a year of it." Somebody has been taking him bread on Sundays, and it's getting harder.
text [cd6088] = 阁楼里有张吊床空着，上面还搭着一件外套。你问了一句。“乔里，”过了一会儿，管着阁楼的南·梅罗说，“在保税仓库那边被逮住了，巡警把他带走了。城里的监狱，一年。”一直有人星期天给他送面包，可越来越难了。
# en: A harbour-town loft with a row of hammocks, one empty with a worn coat hanging over it, a sharp-eyed older woman and a student looking at it.
scene [82e7d3] = 港镇阁楼里一排吊床，其中一张空着，上面搭着件旧外套，一位目光锐利的年长女人和一个学生看着它。
# en: Take him the bread yourself
opt.left.label [43479a] = 亲自给他送面包
# en: You carry the loft's bread to the gaol yourself and wait out the visiting queue. Jory is thinner and pretends he isn't. He wants to hear about magic, all of it, and listens with his face to the bars like someone at a sweetshop window. Leaving him there is the hardest thing you do all break.
opt.left.say.1 [b22177] = 你亲自把阁楼的面包带到监狱，排完了探视的长队。乔里瘦了，却装作没瘦。他想听魔法的事，所有的事，脸贴着栏杆听，像个趴在糖果店橱窗上的孩子。把他留在那里，是你整个假期里最难做的事。
# en: Pay his bread for the year
opt.right.label [938fe8] = 付清他一年的面包钱
# en: You give Nan enough to keep Jory in bread and blankets for the rest of his year. She counts it twice and puts it away without thanks, which is how the loft says it. That night somebody has mended your coat.
opt.right.say.1 [b7ea3c] = 你给了南足够的钱，让乔里余下的刑期里不缺面包和毯子。她数了两遍，收起来，没道谢——阁楼里就是这么道谢的。那天夜里，有人把你的外套补好了。
